    <h1>About Tibetan Monasteries</h1>
    <p>This project intends to explore the relationships between Tibetan Buddhist religious figures and monasteries, and to map the social and geographic networks created by these relationships. Like many religions, Tibetan Buddhism is divided into traditions, which differ in practice, philosophy, organization, and history, and sometimes engage in sectarian rivalries. The primary traditions of Tibetan Buddhism are Geluk, Kagyu, Sakya, and Nyingma. The political and educational institutions of these traditions have historically centered on specific monasteries. For instance the Dalai Lama’s Geluk tradition has its primary monastic and scholarly institutions at Ganden, Drepung, and Sera. Prominent religious figures studied and taught at monasteries of their own tradition, but monastic connections were often much broader than that. Religious figures often undertook studies in an itinerant way, visiting monasteries of traditions other than their own that were reputated for philosophic learning or certain ritual practices. Thus their education and spiritual influences might span multiple traditions. Monasteries were also centers of pilgrimage and seats of political influence, roles that likewise could transcend sectarian boundaries.</p>
